   "title": "Why a 660-ton ball makes Taipei 101 safer",
   "url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oaRu-RZbYTw",
   "published": "2026-11-01",
   "summary": "Why does Taipei 101 hang a 660-ton ball near the top? The ball is 5.5 meters across, built from 41 layers of steel plate, each 12.5 centimeters thick, and the official site puts it at 660 metric tons. It hangs on cables, and when wind pushes the tower one way, the ball swings against it. That's called a tuned mass damper. The official site says its main purpose is to reduce the swaying of the tower during strong winds. Same weight, but free to swing, it protects the building. Idea and structure: Mark Rober's Short \"How To Keep a Skyscraper From Falling Over\". Sources are in the pinned comment. Photos: Wpcpey (CC BY 4.0), Chaplain iMonk and Richard Bitting (CC BY 2.0), via Wikimedia Commons. Footage: NASA #shorts #science #engineering"
  },
